{"label":"concave","explanation":"f(x) = 1 / (7/4 + e^x) on [1,2]. Let g(x) = 7/4 + e^x, so f(x) = g(x)^{-1}. Then f'(x) = -g'(x)g(x)^{-2} = -e^x(7/4 + e^x)^{-2}. Differentiating again,

f''(x) = -[e^x(7/4 + e^x)^{-2}]' = -[e^x(7/4 + e^x)^{-2} - 2e^{2x}(7/4 + e^x)^{-3}] = -e^x(7/4 + e^x)^{-3}[(7/4 + e^x) - 2e^x] = -e^x(7/4 + e^x)^{-3}(7/4 - e^x).

For x ∈ [1,2], e^x ≥ e^1 > 7/4, so (7/4 - e^x) < 0 while e^x > 0 and (7/4 + e^x)^{-3} > 0. Thus the product inside is negative, and the leading minus sign makes f''(x) > 0 on [1,2]. Therefore f is strictly convex and not concave on [1,2]."}

{"label":"convex","explanation":"f(x) = 1 / ((7/4) + e^x) on [-2, -1]. Let g(x) = (7/4) + e^x, so f(x) = g(x)^{-1}. Then g'(x) = e^x and g''(x) = e^x, and on the whole interval we have g(x) > 0, g'(x) > 0, g''(x) > 0. Using the derivatives directly, f'(x) = -g'(x)/g(x)^2 and f''(x) = (2(g'(x))^2 - g(x)g''(x)) / g(x)^3. Substituting g and its derivatives gives

f''(x) = [2(e^x)^2 - ((7/4)+e^x)e^x]/((7/4)+e^x)^3 = e^x(2e^x - (7/4 + e^x))/((7/4)+e^x)^3 = e^x(e^x - 7/4)/((7/4)+e^x)^3.

On [-2, -1], e^x ∈ [e^{-2}, e^{-1}] ⊂ (0, 1) and is always less than 7/4, so e^x - 7/4 < 0 while e^x > 0 and (7/4 + e^x)^3 > 0. Thus the numerator e^x(e^x - 7/4) is negative and the denominator positive, giving f''(x) < 0 for all x in [-2, -1]. Therefore f is strictly concave and not convex on this interval."}

